  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 311 PM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nAn active long term weather pattern looks to be \ntaking shape. At the upper levels, a strong ridge takes hold of the \nWestern United states limiting our overall wind shear potential \nthrough the weekend. Between the two low pressure systems progged to \nmove along the Canadian border, our ridge will limit their influence \nsomewhat, but continued light monsoonal moisture advecting into the \narea will make it interesting. The overall forecast will be pretty \nmuch the same thing will about a 40 percent chance of storms in the \nafternoon and evening across the area and breezy but calmer \nmornings. 700mb temperatures look to stay between 13 and 17C meaning \nthe daily high temperatures will be in the 80's for Southeast \nWyoming and 90's in the Nebraska Panhandle especially towards the \nend of the week. There will be a cool down as showers and \nthunderstorms move over an area so some relief will occur for those \nwho aren't a fan of the summer heat. The Wyoming wind will also \nstart to make an appearance later this week. While not quite high \nwinds yet (except under thunderstorms). The normal afternoon breezes \nwill steadily become a little stronger Friday afternoon and into the \nweekend. These winds are induced from the slightly tightened \npressure gradient as an upper level trough pushes against our ridge \nabout as well as dragging anything across sand paper. So expect \nbreezier conditions especially in the mountains west of I-25 and \nalong I-80.",
